{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at exact clean head 2520cffdcdf1bda5ccf617159136952227985090 (branch request/railway-record-framing), answering request cc6d71ea under promise 24ddd696. This artifact is the implementation report.\n\nThe defect, reproduced before I promised rather than taken from the description. graph.go framed records with %x1f between fields and %x1e between records. Four of the eight fields are committer-controlled - subject, author name, body, and the Rests-On trailer value - and git stores those bytes verbatim. A two-commit repository whose second body carried a record separator plus a forged field layout rendered three commits through the real Store.Graph:\n\n  [1] hash=\"FORGEDHASH\" subject=\"FORGED SUBJECT\" author=\"Evil Author\" time=9999999999\n\nThat row does not exist. Anyone able to land a commit message - here, anyone who can push a branch - could author graph entries with a hash, subject, author and timestamp of their choosing, and the far-future timestamp placed the forgery wherever they wanted it in the ordering.\n\nThe fix rests on an invariant git enforces rather than one we hope for. I checked it directly rather than assuming: git refuses a NUL byte in a commit log message, and in an ident. So no committer-controlled field can emit the separator. Fields are NUL-separated and read at a fixed stride of eight, which nothing a commit can carry will desynchronise. Re-running the same exploit against this head returns two commits for two commits with no forged row.\n\nTwo silent failures became refusals, and both mattered. A short record hit len(fields) \u003c 8 and was skipped, so malformed or truncated history disappeared from the railway rather than announcing itself. An unparsable timestamp left Time at zero, which sorted that commit to the front - exactly where a hostile timestamp would aim it. A view that quietly omits or misplaces history is worse than one that refuses to render.\n\nparseGraphStream is split from the command that feeds it so those refusal paths are directly reachable in tests. They are the branches that used to fail silently, and a test forced to stage a malformed repository to reach them would not have reached them at all.\n\nOne subtlety that is easy to get backwards, so it is pinned by a test. Only the trailing empty token that -z leaves after the final record is dropped. An empty field in the middle is legitimate - a commit with no decorations or no trailers has both - and discarding those would desynchronise the very stride the framing depends on, reintroducing the defect inside its own fix.\n\nEvidence, not assertion. Six hostile-metadata cases - separators in body, in subject, in author name, in a Rests-On trailer, unit separators throughout a body, and terminal controls with newlines - plus the honest round-trip all fail when the old separators are restored, so they catch the real defect rather than merely passing. Three mutations of the new guards, each verified to have applied before the run, each caught: skipping malformed records instead of refusing (3 tests), tolerating a bad timestamp (2), and dropping every empty field rather than only the terminator (13).\n\nGates at this exact head: go test ./... with 29 packages passing, go vet clean, make docs clean, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean.\n\nScope: internal/gitstore only. Ordinary Graph output semantics and refs handling are unchanged, which the round-trip test pins on exact bytes of subject, body and trailer.","body":{"commit":"2520cffdcdf1bda5ccf617159136952227985090","path":"internal/gitstore"}}